{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>דלף</b> — it is the dripping of rain, as in: ידלוף הבית (the house drips)." "<b>טורד</b> — drives away, as in Targum: ויגרשום וטרדינון (and they drove them out and expelled them)." "<b>סגריר</b> — reduplication of the lamed [i.e. the middle root letter], on the pattern זרזיף, meaning a day when people shut themselves in because of the dripping rain that drives them away and expels them — or the rain itself is called סגריר." "<b>ואשת מדינים</b> — she is compared to dripping rain, for she is driven away from her neighbors. And one who wishes to hide her so that he not send quarrels — let him hide wind and breath. Even if his right hand anoints her with oil of myrrh to call to her to seduce her — it is wind, for he cannot discipline her, and the turnings of the wind are many, and man has no ability to seize hold of it. Therefore he likened the woman to wind; and in the matter that the right hand is prepared for anything, he said: ושמן ימינו (and the oil of his right hand). Similarly: הנה ימינך (behold Your right hand)."], :book :proverbs, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {2 "Ibn Ezra writes 'the lamed' (הלמ\"ד) to indicate the middle radical is doubled, per the standard grammatical terminology where lamed = third position in a three-letter root; here the root ס-ג-ר has its lamed (ג) doubled."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Proverbs -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/mishley/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:25:10.067300843Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}